Explanation: Hoisting in JavaScript
Hoisting is JavaScript's behavior of moving declarations (not initializations) to the top of their scope at runtime.


Keyword	Hoisted?	Initialized?	Accessible Before Declaration?
var	✅ Yes	✔ Initialized to undefined	✅ Yes, outputs undefined
let	✅ Yes	❌ Not initialized	❌ No, causes ReferenceError (TDZ)
const	✅ Yes	❌ Not initialized	❌ No, causes ReferenceError (TDZ)
var is hoisted and initialized to undefined. Accessing it before declaration doesn’t throw an error but returns undefined.

let and const are hoisted but not initialized. They remain in a temporal dead zone (TDZ) until their declaration line is executed, so accessing them before declaration throws a ReferenceError.